From a1532c36c99a92a96fbfeb62fdb736c6cf4e5a13 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 15 Jul 2026 03:57:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07180/11362] [outer-loop] fix --update-baseline's cherry-pick
 guard to tolerate bonus solves; update baseline to today's real 7/8 result

Fourth fix in the submission-prep session. After the three prior fixes
(620bf5f65, c15dcb972, cc87202d3) landed, a real submission scored
0.12 on the leaderboard -- a genuine improvement over every prior
submission's 0.08. Per the user's standing instruction, ran
--update-baseline to lock in the confirmed result.

It refused: validate_canonical_baseline's cherry-pick guard requires
the baseline's core-identity field to equal exactly CANONICAL_CORE_GAMES
(4 games), but the real measurement solved 7/8 (4 core + 3 bonus:
cd82/ft09/su15) and --update-baseline naively persisted the full
solved_games (7 entries) as the new baseline's core identity. The
--check path already handles bonus solves correctly (reported
separately, never netted against core); --update-baseline just didn't
apply the same scoping when building the new baseline dict.

Fixed by intersecting the persisted solved_games with
CANONICAL_CORE_GAMES before validation, so bonus solves are preserved
in the baseline's other fields (actions_by_game, efficiency_by_game)
but never block the core-identity check. Verified: baseline now updates
successfully (solved 7, median actions/solve 7792), and a new
regression test proves a 7-game bonus-inclusive measurement is accepted
while still persisting exactly the canonical 4 as solved_games.

ops/arc-submission-baseline.json now reflects today's real, stronger
measurement (sp80 efficiency 0.0001->0.0264, vc33 0.0001->0.3785,
plus per-level efficiency/deepest-level/reset-replay/forward-walk
fields for every game) as the new floor for future gate checks.
